true

# 10c. THE PROFILED SHAPE — a PARTITIONED table whose projection names NO partition column takes the
#      plain form. This is where the whole finding lives: the live query that went 291 s -> 77 s reads
#      three non-partition columns of a table whose files sit under edwYear=…/edwMonth=…, and it was
#      paying the full form's two footer sweeps for nothing. Without this assertion, gating partitioned
#      tables off the plain form entirely would leave 10a passing.
#      ⚠ The reverse direction is pinned right after it, and it is a REAL RESIDUAL rather than a
#      tidiness note: projecting a partition column KEEPS the full form, so `SELECT *` on a partitioned
#      table still pays the sweeps. The value is absent from the data files, so reaching it needs a
#      per-file join key (`filename`) — and ⚠ `filename => true` DOES compose with the VARCHAR-keyed map
#      (measured 2026-08-15; an earlier version of this note claimed it refused), so the residual is
#      UNIMPLEMENTED rather than blocked: the fix is the full form's per-file constants join brought over
#      to the plain form. (`schema` + `hive_partitioning` together stay refused outright.)
#      ⚠ Its own table, NOT §6's `parts`: that one is UPDATEd and DELETEd from, so by here it carries a
#      DELETION VECTOR — which routes to the full form for a reason that has nothing to do with
#      partitioning and would make this section prove the opposite of what it says.
query II
SELECT count(*), sum(id) FROM b.main.rparts;
----
60	1770

query I
SELECT count(*) > 0 FROM duckdb_logs
WHERE type LIKE 'Fabricator.Delta%' AND message LIKE '%delta native batch%'
  AND message LIKE '%rparts%' AND message LIKE '%schema = map%';
----
true

# Projecting the partition column (the filter forces it into the projection) keeps the full form.
query II
SELECT count(*), sum(id) FROM b.main.rparts WHERE p = 'p1';
----
20	590

query I
SELECT count(*) > 0 FROM duckdb_logs
WHERE type LIKE 'Fabricator.Delta%' AND message LIKE '%delta native batch%'
  AND message LIKE '%rparts%' AND message LIKE '%union_by_name%';
----
true

# ============================================================================
# 11. THE PUSHED BARE LIMIT REACHES THE GENERATED SQL (2026-08-15). Before this, `ScanSpec.Top` was
#     consumed by SqlServerBackend alone: a `LIMIT 1` over a Delta table read every file and discarded
#     the rows above the scan (~25 s of a 71 s statement on the profiled 89-file remote table — and with
#     the plain form's DECLARED schema, footers are read at execution, so the limit also caps how many
#     files are OPENED at all). Now the suffix is appended to the batch query and to each per-file query
#     (a per-file LIMIT is a SUPERSET — each file capped at n, DuckDB re-limits above).
#     The observable is the generated SQL through the same Debug lines as §10, plus the scan Info line's
#     new `top=` field — a row-count assertion passes either way, since DuckDB re-limits. Each LIMIT
#     value below is UNIQUE to its statement so the accumulated log cannot satisfy an assertion with an
#     earlier statement's line.
#     ⚠ EVERY SHAPE HERE WAS PROBED BEFORE BEING PINNED, because the first draft asserted a plan that
#     does not exist: `count(*) FROM (SELECT id FROM t LIMIT n)` is NOT a batched projection scan — the
#     optimizer turns it into the count-via-rowid plan (`cols=[] rowid=True`), which LOOPS per file. That
#     accident is kept below as the LOOP leg's fixture. A bare projection under LIMIT must return
#     order-independent VALUES to be assertable (`id IS NOT NULL`), since a bare LIMIT's rows are
#     arbitrary.
#     ⚠ The NEGATIVE control (§11d) is the load-bearing half and carries a POSITIVE witness so it cannot
#     hold vacuously: a FILTERED limit must NOT push — arrow_ingest emits "top" only when the scan carries
#     no filter (a best-effort filter's superset plus an early limit could starve real matches) —
#     witnessed by the scan line showing the filter WITH `top=-`.
#     ⚠ This section is about the BARE limit only. A limit arriving WITH an order is TopN pushdown and
#     lives in §12; until 2026-08-16 it was a second negative here (the reader declined it, since an
#     unordered limit trims arbitrary rows), and that expectation is now inverted — the two must be read
#     and edited together.
# ============================================================================

# 11a. Plain form carries the limit (schema-map SQL ends in it), and the spec carried top=3.
query I
SELECT id IS NOT NULL FROM b.main.route_plain LIMIT 3;
----
true
true
true

query I
SELECT count(*) > 0 FROM duckdb_logs
WHERE type LIKE 'Fabricator.Delta%' AND message LIKE '%delta native batch%'
  AND message LIKE '%route_plain%' AND message LIKE '%schema = map%' AND message LIKE '%LIMIT 3%';
----
true

query I
SELECT count(*) > 0 FROM duckdb_logs
WHERE type LIKE 'Fabricator.Delta%' AND message LIKE '%delta native scan%'
  AND message LIKE '%route_plain%' AND message LIKE '%top=3%';
----
true

# 11b. The UNION form carries it too — appended AFTER the last branch, so it limits the WHOLE union,
#      not one branch (a per-branch cap would also be superset-safe, but whole-union is exact).
#      ⚠ THIS SECTION IS ALSO THE GATE FOR THE BATCH QUERY'S OWNERSHIP HAND-OFF, which is not obvious from
#      what it asserts. A pushed LIMIT makes the consumer stop reading EARLY, and the pump is not joined on
#      teardown — so it keeps draining in the background while the scan stream is disposed. If anything ever
#      releases the pre-opened batch query from the consumer side (see BatchQueryOwner.Claim), that frees a
#      DuckDB result the pump is still reading: MEASURED as STATUS_HEAP_CORRUPTION (0xC0000374), a silent
#      process death here with no assertion output and no stack. A bound deletion vector is what makes it
#      reproduce reliably, which is why it lands on this section and not on §11a's plain form.
query I
SELECT id IS NOT NULL FROM b.main.route_dv LIMIT 5;
----
true
true
true
true
true

query I
SELECT count(*) > 0 FROM duckdb_logs
WHERE type LIKE 'Fabricator.Delta%' AND message LIKE '%delta native batch%'
  AND message LIKE '%route_dv%' AND message LIKE '% UNION ALL %' AND message LIKE '%LIMIT 5%';
----
true

# ...and the FULL form (union_by_name) as well, reached via a projected rowid beside a data column
# (probed: cols=[rowid,id] top=5 routes there; a rowid-ONLY projection would loop instead).
query II
SELECT r >= 0, id IS NOT NULL FROM (SELECT rowid AS r, id FROM b.main.route_dv LIMIT 5) t;
----
true	true
true	true
true	true
true	true
true	true

query I
SELECT count(*) > 0 FROM duckdb_logs
WHERE type LIKE 'Fabricator.Delta%' AND message LIKE '%delta native batch%'
  AND message LIKE '%route_dv%' AND message LIKE '%union_by_name%' AND message LIKE '%LIMIT 5%';
----
true

# 11c. The PER-FILE LOOP carries it as well — via the count-via-rowid plan (see the header), which
#      declines both batch forms (`rowid=True`) and loops every file. Only the FIRST file's Debug line is
#      deterministic (the statement can end before later files are scheduled), so assert > 0, never a
#      count.
query I
SELECT count(*) FROM (SELECT id FROM b.main.route_plain LIMIT 9) t;
